### How does setPeriodic scheduling work?

For simple fixed-rate scheduling, `vertx.setPeriodic(interval, handler)` fires every interval milliseconds and returns a timer id you can cancel. The handler executes on the event loop, so it must not block.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import io.vertx.core.AbstractVerticle;
import io.vertx.core.Vertx;

public class PeriodicTrigger extends AbstractVerticle {
    @Override
    public void start() {
        // Fire every 60 seconds
        long timerId = vertx.setPeriodic(60_000, id -> {
            System.out.println("Trigger fired, delegating to worker...");
            // Kick off heavy work on the worker pool
            vertx.executeBlocking(promise -> {
                reconcileAccounts();
                promise.complete();
            });
        });

        System.out.println("Reconciliation scheduled, timer id: " + timerId);

        // Cancel after 10 minutes
        vertx.setTimer(600_000, id -> {
            vertx.cancelTimer(timerId);
            System.out.println("Timer cancelled");
        });
    }

    private void reconcileAccounts() {
        System.out.println("Reconciling accounts for National Bank of India...");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vertx.vertx().deployVerticle(new PeriodicTrigger());
    }
}
```

```text
Reconciliation scheduled, timer id: 1
Trigger fired, delegating to worker...
Reconciling accounts for National Bank of India...
```

### How do worker verticles handle batch processing?

A worker verticle is deployed with `setWorker(true)` and runs entirely on worker threads, so its handler may block. For batch jobs, you trigger it over the event bus.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import io.vertx.core.AbstractVerticle;
import io.vertx.core.DeploymentOptions;
import io.vertx.core.Vertx;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class InterestBatchVerticle extends AbstractVerticle {
    @Override
    public void start() {
        // This runs on a worker thread: blocking is allowed
        vertx.eventBus().consumer("batch.interest", msg -> {
            System.out.println("Starting interest calculation...");
            BigDecimal totalInterest = calculateDailyInterest();
            msg.reply(totalInterest.toString());
        });
    }

    private BigDecimal calculateDailyInterest() {
        // Simulates processing 10,000 accounts
        BigDecimal total = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        BigDecimal rate = new BigDecimal("0.04"); // 4% annual
        BigDecimal dailyRate = rate.divide(
                new BigDecimal("365"), 10, BigDecimal.ROUND_HALF_UP);

        for (int i = 0; i < 10_000; i++) {
            BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("100000.00");
            BigDecimal interest = balance.multiply(dailyRate);
            total = total.add(interest);
        }
        System.out.println("Processed 10,000 accounts");
        return total;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vertx vertx = Vertx.vertx();

        // Deploy as worker verticle
        vertx.deployVerticle(new InterestBatchVerticle(),
                new DeploymentOptions().setWorker(true))
                .onSuccess(id -> {
                    // Trigger the batch job over the event bus
                    vertx.eventBus().request("batch.interest", "", reply -> {
                        if (reply.succeeded()) {
                            System.out.println("Total interest: " + reply.result().body());
                        }
                    });
                });
    }
}
```

```text
Starting interest calculation...
Processed 10,000 accounts
Total interest: 10958.9041095890
```

### How does executeBlocking differ from a worker verticle?

`executeBlocking` is simpler for one-off tasks, while a worker verticle is better for a dedicated long-running service.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import io.vertx.core.AbstractVerticle;
import io.vertx.core.Vertx;
import io.vertx.core.WorkerExecutor;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ExecuteBlockingDemo extends AbstractVerticle {
    @Override
    public void start() {
        // Dedicated worker pool for batch work
        WorkerExecutor batchPool = vertx.createSharedWorkerExecutor(
                "batch-pool", 10);

        // Run on the dedicated pool
        batchPool.executeBlocking(promise -> {
            BigDecimal result = processStatements();
            promise.complete(result);
        }).onSuccess(total ->
                System.out.println("Statement batch complete: " + total));
    }

    private BigDecimal processStatements() {
        System.out.println("Processing statements on thread: "
                + Thread.currentThread().getName());
        return new BigDecimal("5000000.00");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vertx.vertx().deployVerticle(new ExecuteBlockingDemo());
    }
}
```

```text
Processing statements on thread: vert.x-worker-thread-0
Statement batch complete: 5000000.00
```

### How do you implement cron scheduling?

For calendar-style cron scheduling, `setPeriodic` is not enough because it only does fixed intervals. Parse a cron expression, compute the delay until the next fire time, and arm a timer.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import io.vertx.core.AbstractVerticle;
import io.vertx.core.Vertx;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.time.LocalTime;
import java.time.Duration;

public class CronScheduler extends AbstractVerticle {
    private static final LocalTime TARGET_TIME = LocalTime.of(2, 0); // 2 AM

    @Override
    public void start() {
        scheduleNext();
    }

    private void scheduleNext() {
        long delayMs = computeDelayUntilNext();
        System.out.println("Next batch in " + (delayMs / 1000) + " seconds");

        vertx.setTimer(delayMs, id -> {
            System.out.println("2 AM batch triggered at " + LocalDateTime.now());
            // Run heavy work on worker pool
            vertx.executeBlocking(promise -> {
                runEndOfDayBatch();
                promise.complete();
            }).onComplete(ar -> scheduleNext()); // re-arm
        });
    }

    private long computeDelayUntilNext() {
        LocalDateTime now = LocalDateTime.now();
        LocalDateTime next = now.toLocalDate().atTime(TARGET_TIME);
        if (now.isAfter(next)) {
            next = next.plusDays(1);
        }
        return Duration.between(now, next).toMillis();
    }

    private void runEndOfDayBatch() {
        System.out.println("Running end-of-day interest posting "
                + "for National Bank of India...");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vertx.vertx().deployVerticle(new CronScheduler());
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes with batch jobs in Vert.x?

The most common mistake is running the batch work directly on the event loop.

```java
// WRONG: blocking the event loop with batch processing
vertx.setPeriodic(60_000, id -> {
    // This processes 100,000 accounts ON the event loop
    for (int i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) {
        processAccount(i); // blocks!
    }
});
```

```java
// CORRECT: delegate to a worker
vertx.setPeriodic(60_000, id -> {
    vertx.executeBlocking(promise -> {
        for (int i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) {
            processAccount(i); // safe on worker thread
        }
        promise.complete();
    });
});
```

### How do you prevent duplicate execution in a cluster?

In a clustered deployment, every node receives the same timer tick, so you must prevent all nodes from running the same batch job. Use leader election or a distributed lock.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import io.vertx.core.AbstractVerticle;
import io.vertx.core.shareddata.Lock;

public class ClusterSafeBatch extends AbstractVerticle {
    @Override
    public void start() {
        vertx.setPeriodic(60_000, id -> {
            // Try to acquire a cluster-wide lock
            vertx.sharedData().getLockWithTimeout(
                    "batch.interest.lock", 1000, lockResult -> {
                if (lockResult.succeeded()) {
                    Lock lock = lockResult.result();
                    System.out.println("This node won the lock, running batch");
                    vertx.executeBlocking(promise -> {
                        runInterestBatch();
                        promise.complete();
                    }).onComplete(ar -> lock.release());
                } else {
                    System.out.println("Another node is running the batch");
                }
            });
        });
    }

    private void runInterestBatch() {
        System.out.println("Posting daily interest...");
    }
}
```

### What about idempotency and chunking?

Long batch jobs should be idempotent and chunked so a crash mid-batch can be recovered safely.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ChunkedBatch {
    private static final int CHUNK_SIZE = 1000;

    public void processAllAccounts(int totalAccounts) {
        int offset = 0;
        while (offset < totalAccounts) {
            int end = Math.min(offset + CHUNK_SIZE, totalAccounts);
            processChunk(offset, end);
            // Commit per chunk so a crash loses at most one chunk
            System.out.println("Committed chunk " + offset + " to " + end);
            offset = end;
        }
    }

    private void processChunk(int start, int end) {
        BigDecimal rate = new BigDecimal("0.04");
        BigDecimal dailyRate = rate.divide(
                new BigDecimal("365"), 10, BigDecimal.ROUND_HALF_UP);
        for (int i = start; i < end; i++) {
            // Idempotent: re-running the same chunk produces the same result
            BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("100000.00");
            BigDecimal interest = balance.multiply(dailyRate);
        }
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Size the worker pool deliberately with `createSharedWorkerExecutor` and monitor event-loop lag.
- For very large data sets, prefer streaming and chunking over loading everything into memory.
- Use the `ordered` parameter of `executeBlocking` to control whether multiple invocations run in parallel.
- In production, monitor batch duration and set alerts when jobs exceed expected time.

```java
// Parallel execution: false means do NOT require ordered execution
vertx.executeBlocking(promise -> {
    processAllAccounts();
    promise.complete();
}, false); // multiple batches can run in parallel
```
